module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveBundleMorphism
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.Generators
import WeilConjectures.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProj.Functoriality
import WeilConjectures.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProj.BaseChange
import WeilConjectures.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProj.Universal
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjectiveSpace
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.SymmetricAlgebra.Basis

/-!
# H-projectivity of a finitely globally generated projective bundle

Source: Stacks Project, Morphisms, Tag 087S, proof paragraph beginning
"If E is generated by finitely many global sections"
(blueprint: lem-trf-projective-bundle-global-generated-h-projective).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The projective bundle of a finite type quasi-coherent module generated by finitely
many global sections is H-projective, in the finite-projective-space convention.
Source: Stacks Project, Morphisms, Tag 087S, proof paragraph beginning
"If E is generated by finitely many global sections"
(blueprint: lem-trf-projective-bundle-global-generated-h-projective).

Proof:
1. Choose the finite generating family from `hgen`, padding by a zero section if it is
empty. Its evaluation map from a finite free module is an epimorphism
[def-weil-pbc-generating-sections]. The symmetric-algebra universal property and affine
comparison [lem-weil-pbc-quasi-coherent-symmetric-algebra] give the induced graded map.
Products of degree-one sections span each symmetric degree
[lem-weil-pbc-symmetric-algebra-graded]. Express these sections locally in the chosen
finite generators to see that the map is surjective as a map of sheaves in every degree.
2. Relative-Proj functoriality [prop-nagata-relative-proj-functoriality] gives a closed
immersion into the relative projective space of the finite free module
[def-nagata-relative-projective-space], compatible with the structural maps
[def-nagata-relative-proj].
3. Over Spec Z the symmetric algebra of the free module is the polynomial algebra
[lem-weil-pbc-symmetric-algebra-free-module-polynomial], preserving symmetric degree.
The affine comparison [lemma-nagata-relative-proj-exists] identifies its relative Proj
with the absolute Proj [def-nagata-affine-proj]. Coefficientwise pullback
[def-nagata-graded-algebra-pullback-data] along S to Spec Z preserves the free symmetric
algebra and every graded piece. Arbitrary relative-Proj base change
[lemma-nagata-relative-proj-base-change-arbitrary] identifies the relative projective
space with the base-change projective space [def-nagata-projective-space-base-change],
using [def-pc-fibre-product]. Compose the closed immersion of Step 2 with this isomorphism
[def-weil-closed-immersion]. Its factorization of the structural morphism is precisely
H-projectivity [def-kun-projective]. -/
theorem isHProjective_projectiveBundle_of_finitely_generated_globalSections
    (S : Scheme.{u}) (E : S.Modules) [E.IsQuasicoherent] [E.IsFiniteType]
    (hgen : ∃ σ : SheafOfModules.GeneratingSections E, σ.IsFiniteType) :
    IsHProjective (SymmetricAlgebraSheaf.gradedAlgebraData E).relProjHom := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
